For a production/support/DevOps/SRE interview, I would explain your incident like this.

1. Strong 2–3 minute interview answer
Production Incident Interview Answer


One of the major production incidents I handled was after an upgrade of XL-Release from version 25.3.3 to 25.3.4.

The issue was detected after the production upgrade on April 22. Several release pipelines started failing because the connections for our custom plugins were missing. Most of the custom plugin connections were lost, while the JIRA plugin connection was still available.

The impact was significant because multiple production release pipelines depended on these custom plugin integrations, so their deployments were blocked.

My first step was to understand the scope of the issue. I checked the failed pipeline logs, verified the plugin configurations through the XL-Release UI, and checked the connection mappings at both global and folder levels. We found that the plugin connections and their mappings to templates were missing after the upgrade.

For immediate mitigation, we worked with the team and vendor to identify whether the issue was related to the upgrade itself or configuration. We reviewed the upgrade history and extracted the plugin connection metadata from the database. Using that information, we recreated the missing connections and re-associated them with the affected templates. We also identified credential dependencies and asked users to reconfigure sensitive credentials such as API tokens and passwords.

During the RCA, we found that the main root cause was a plugin synchronization failure during application startup after the upgrade.

The contributing factor was an incorrect ECR repository endpoint configured during the production upgrade. A test ECR endpoint had been inadvertently configured instead of the production endpoint. This resulted in repeated upgrade and cleanup attempts. During pod initialization, the custom plugins were not properly loaded and synchronized with the database, which caused the existing connections to appear as missing.

We also engaged the vendor, Digital.ai, who confirmed that the plugin synchronization failure was consistent with the observed behavior and that repeated upgrade and cleanup cycles could contribute to partial or failed plugin synchronization.

Once we understood the root cause, we restored the missing connections, remapped them to the templates, validated the affected pipelines, and confirmed that the release functionality was restored.

The important lesson from this incident was that the technical failure was not just the plugin itself; it was also a change-management and validation gap. So, as preventive actions, we introduced mandatory validation of production runbooks, segregation of test and production runbooks, pre-execution checklist and approval, formal change sign-off for upgrade activities, controlled execution instead of ad-hoc commands, and enhanced post-upgrade sanity testing.

Overall, I would describe this incident as a combination of an upgrade-related plugin synchronization failure and an environment configuration mistake. The key things I contributed were troubleshooting the production impact, identifying the missing connection mappings, supporting recovery using database metadata, coordinating with the vendor, and helping define preventive controls so that the same issue would not happen again.

You can make this much stronger in an interview by clearly separating what happened from what you personally did.

2. If the interviewer asks: "What exactly was the root cause?"
Say:

"The immediate technical root cause was a plugin synchronization failure during XL-Release startup after the upgrade. The underlying contributing cause was that the production upgrade was pointing to a test ECR repository endpoint. This caused repeated upgrade and cleanup operations, and eventually the custom plugins were not correctly synchronized with the database. Because of that, the existing plugin connections appeared to be missing, which caused the dependent pipelines to fail."

Then add:

"So I would classify it as a technical synchronization failure triggered by an environment configuration error, with a process gap around pre-upgrade validation."

That last sentence is important because it shows root-cause thinking rather than simply blaming the tool or vendor.

